  "historical_output_text": " A common weather pattern is a heatwave. When hot air builds up over a region, it becomes very warm and dry. The air is stable and any cloud that forms quickly blows away, so the sun shines and heats the ground even more. As the ground gets hot, it heats the air above it. This warm air rises, creating a low pressure area near the ground. Air from cooler regions flows in to replace it. The cool air warms up where it meets the hot air and a front forms. It stops the cold air from pushing in and keeps the heatwave going. As the days pass, the hot air continues to rise and more cool air flows in. Eventually the air in cooler regions also warms up and the heatwave ends. During a heatwave, people need to stay cool and hydrated, and buildings need to be air",
